cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Value-Field Theory (VFT)"
message: "If you use this theory or repository, please cite this repository."
authors:
  - family-names: "Nuno"
    given-names: "T."
    affiliation: "Independent Researcher"
abstract: >
  Value-Field Theory (VFT) is a structural framework for describing how a
  shared multidimensional real-resource world K, actor-specific accessible
  portions K_i, and actor-specific expectation-forming states P_i condition
  actions and decisions A_i. K is not treated as a separate resource world for
  each actor; actor relativity enters through access to K, through P_i, and
  through actor-specific expected interval changes E_i[Delta K]. P retains
  future outlooks, evaluations, preferences, credit, beliefs, and related
  structures. The term expectation stock refers to this expectation-generating
  role rather than to a stored point expectation. E_i[Delta K] is represented
  separately as an actor-specific desired or expected resource change over a
  specified target and interval, not as a required intermediate variable in
  action generation and not as a rate flow. Realized Delta K is kept distinct:
  constraints, interactions, institutions, and other actors can prevent the
  expected change from being fully realized. Actions are a principal agentic
  route for changes in K and P, including policy, communication, observation,
  and information reception. Small non-agentic changes in P need not be denied,
  but are not modeled as separate core paths unless they create meaningful
  observable differences. Economic equilibrium, expected surplus aggregation,
  and real-nominal mappings are treated as domain-specific projection
  candidates rather than VFT-specific universal laws. Where exchange or
  agreement makes heterogeneous expected resource changes comparable within a
  common aggregation scope, aggregate E_i[Delta K] may be projected to expected
  total surplus; this is distinct from realized aggregate Delta K. Observable
  actions, Delta K, Delta P, and nominal-economic indicators may be used as
  proxies subject to domain-specific measurement assumptions.
repository-code: "https://github.com/nuno929/vf-model"
license: "CC-BY-4.0"
keywords:
  - value-field theory
  - multidimensional resource state
